        "id": 236367,
        "name": "catDreaming",
        "username": "catDreaming",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/c/4491bb/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2021-03-27T05:05:17.023Z",
        "cooked": "<p>It recently came up in a Sentinels RPG Game that I play in that Push Your Limits might be interpreted in different ways.</p>\n<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Ability Text\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n Ability Text:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Push Your Limits (I): You have no limit on the number of reactions you can take. Each time you use a Reaction after the first one each turn, take 1 irreducible damage or take a minor twist.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>The following text is a summary of how I play it out in game, when Push Your Limits is active.</p>\n<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Catdreaming's Implementation\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n Catdreaming's Implementation:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>When Push Your Limits is active, you gain the following benefits:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Your Reaction refreshes at the start of every turn, instead of refreshing at the start of your turn. <span class=\"bbcode-i\">You do not gain extra uses of your Reaction if it is refreshed without being used prior.</span></li>\n<li>You may use Reactions even if yours has been used already. When you use a Reaction this way, first take 1 irreducible damage, or take a minor twist.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>What are other people’s thoughts on the topic?</p>",
        "post_number": 1,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-03-27T05:05:17.023Z",
        "reply_count": 1,
        "reply_to_post_number": null,
        "quote_count": 0,
        "incoming_link_count": 93,
        "reads": 35,
        "readers_count": 34,
        "score": 522.0,
        "yours": false,
