        "bookmarked": false,
        "raw": "It recently came up in a Sentinels RPG Game that I play in that Push Your Limits might be interpreted in different ways.\n[Quote=\"Ability Text\"]\nPush Your Limits (I): You have no limit on the number of reactions you can take. Each time you use a Reaction after the first one each turn, take 1 irreducible damage or take a minor twist.\n[/Quote]\nThe following text is a summary of how I play it out in game, when Push Your Limits is active.\n[Quote=\"Catdreaming's Implementation\"]\nWhen Push Your Limits is active, you gain the following benefits:\n- Your Reaction refreshes at the start of every turn, instead of refreshing at the start of your turn. [I]You do not gain extra uses of your Reaction if it is refreshed without being used prior.[/I]\n- You may use Reactions even if yours has been used already. When you use a Reaction this way, first take 1 irreducible damage, or take a minor twist.\n[/QUOTE]\nWhat are other people's thoughts on the topic?",
